#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "dcd503a8-a9c6-524b-a8ac-0641ce3e1107" :question/math-academy-id "e-12423" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S'$, the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$?\n\n1. $(-2,0)$\n2. $(0,0)$\n3. $(2,0)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0)$, $(0,0)$, and $(2,0)$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "964efb4a-6e6b-5fc1-a993-20aa2b0acdaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-12424" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/71b4c00c3f3f2354e52c4ddadc41fdd1.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2)$, and $(-2,4)$. Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/90a937e06df1fa3177ee4608a88110e5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "4aa087bf-ee6c-5e90-9431-c4be6b207333" :question/math-academy-id "e-12438" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/66b3a70a542c03540cf219bdce0d024d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0$, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0$, we have two additional subcases:If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0)$, the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96613aa1-9c36-500a-a41e-c35cb51432aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102200} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf8242c-8fb2-5898-ad17-c871c5f73bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102201} {:answer/id #uuid "ce929ac1-17f1-5017-a524-3020f0f83977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102202} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aa19f6-551c-5953-ba44-dd77177a2953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102203} {:answer/id #uuid "fb67936e-1532-50e7-8865-84bea44bb18c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7aa19f6-551c-5953-ba44-dd77177a2953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102203} :answer-field/id #uuid "699e658e-8932-5996-9d7e-35e0ceaaf3fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102199}] :question/id #uuid "6dc04420-7edf-5061-a2f1-81e1e58efd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-162145" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Dev